WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.640
Even though Pokémon Go has broken download
records and attracted more players than any

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.530
app in recent history, it didn't take all
that long for fan fervor to die down as frustrations

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.019
with the game have risen.

00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:17.900
From not delivering on promises to outright
cutting out vital parts of the game, here

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.739
are a few reasons once-dedicated players have
turned against Pokémon Go.

00:00:22.739 --> 00:00:24.590
The three-step bug

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:29.259
When Pokémon Go launched, the game included
a feature that allowed players to locate nearby

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:33.960
Pokémon with some accuracy, which was in
line with the app's advertising.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.540
Instead of making players wander aimlessly,
hoping for a random encounter, the game made

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.120
it so little monsters in your vicinity would
show up in a pop-up grid indicating their

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.640
distance from you.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.720
Before long, this feature stopped working,
giving players no clue at all about where

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.220
to find that elusive Snorlax.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.520
That might be fine for a Game Boy game, but
not so much for the real world.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.970
As of the making of this video, the feature
has not returned, with developer Niantic saying

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.440
that it put too much strain on the game's
servers.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.670
Terms of service

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.920
Because the game's tracking was busted, a
website called Pokévision emerged to help

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.820
find nearby Pokemon.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.420
Even though the site broke Niantic's terms
of service by data mining to locate Pokémon,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.450
it provided an extremely useful tool to make
up for a missing feature.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.590
Unfortunately, after a few weeks, Niantic
forced Pokévision to shut down.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:28.979
This was a breaking point for many players,
who weren't shy about voicing their frustration.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:32.970
The developers later explained in a blog post
that the data scraping was still straining

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:38.119
their servers and preventing them from launching
in more countries, so everyone basically had

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:39.950
to take one for the team.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.100
The update

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.950
Around this time, Niantic also pushed a small
update to the game.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:50.700
But rather than fix any outstanding bugs,
like the app constantly freezing or its inaccurate

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.480
GPS, Niantic only made minor cosmetic changes,
and completely removed the game's tracking

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.480
ability.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.340
Angry users started contacting the app stores
for refunds, and Niantic became more hated

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.470
than Mr. Mime, Pokémon's number one crime
against nature.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.869
Players speculated that the minor updates
were done only to bury the app's one-star

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:16.219
ratings, as some online stores only reflect
the ratings of an app's most recent version.

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:20.040
A slight change to the game's code, and Niantic
wiped the slate clean.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.389
Eventually, tracking was kind of replaced,
allowing players to find Pokemon based on

00:02:24.389 --> 00:02:28.540
local Pokestops, but it still wasn't the same.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.059
Microtransaction manipulation

00:02:31.059 --> 00:02:36.510
One game update also conveniently included
a bug that made Pokémon harder to capture.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.799
Some players denied that the game had increased
in difficulty, though seeing a lowly Caterpie

00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:44.569
swat away a perfect throw implied otherwise.

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:49.090
Niantic confirmed the bug, but did not immediately
offer a solution, because it didn't really

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:53.499
do much more than make players purchase more
items from the game's store.

00:02:53.499 --> 00:02:58.349
With trust between players and Niantic already
shaken, many speculated that the change wasn't

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:03.340
a bug at all, but Niantic purposely gaming
the system to make more money.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.129
No reply

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:09.480
Players tried to contact Niantic for solutions,
but discovered that the email address the

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.809
developer had provided for customer support
wasn't being monitored.

00:03:13.809 --> 00:03:18.790
Reddit users coordinated masses of people
in the Pokémon Go community to contact Apple

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.370
and Google and let them know about Niantic's
terms of service violations.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.169
Tips were dropped about how to successfully
request refunds, and Niantic came under full

00:03:28.169 --> 00:03:33.919
attack by a user base that had become upset
with a game that was totally breaking down.

00:03:33.919 --> 00:03:34.919
Shut it down

00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:40.359
By now, Niantic had received complaints about
certain locations not being appropriate for

00:03:40.359 --> 00:03:45.510
Pokémon Go use, from personal residences
to a Holocaust museum.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.749
Players started to see the first big wave
of Pokéstop closures, which became apparent

00:03:49.749 --> 00:03:54.510
when Niantic started shutting down Pokéstops
at childrens' hospitals, where the game had

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:56.939
become especially popular.

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:01.409
Players guessed that the game's Augmented
Reality features could be a violation of HIPAA

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:06.230
regulations, but that didn't do much to quell
the disappointment of the countless kids who

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.199
no longer had an available gym or Pokéstop.

00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:14.239
Once the darling of the app world, Niantic
had entered a PR nightmare worse than those

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:16.329
seizure-inducing Porygon episodes.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:21.910
"We now return to battling seizure robots."

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:22.910
Winter is Coming

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:28.580
Pokémon Go is skewed towards urban players,
since that's where Pokémon spawn most often,

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:34.170
so for many rural players, catching monsters
as a car passenger was really the only option

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.720
to safely build an army of Eevees.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.510
Despite Niantic's on-screen warnings to never
use the app while driving, further updates

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:46.870
completely removed the ability to catch anything
at driving, or even biking, speeds.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:52.220
For people without sidewalks and kids on long,
boring car rides, the game was essentially

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.220
killed.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.290
And even though Niantic had already lost a
record number of players, those numbers will

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:02.470
continue to drop during colder weather, since
no one will want to take off their gloves

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:04.490
to catch another dumb Pidgey.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.340
Terrible GPS

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:11.710
One of Pokémon Go's more rewarding features
is the ability for players to hatch eggs by

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.290
walking large distances.

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:18.650
Not only does the feature really get people
outside and on their feet, but it also promises

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.170
better Pokémon for your efforts.

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:25.980
That is, it would — if the game's GPS worked
consistently.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:30.490
Niantic continues to struggle with mapping
players' walking distances accurately, with

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:35.330
many players reporting that fewer than half
of their steps have been tracked after a hike.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:40.540
With the GPS repeatedly cutting out and disconnecting,
players have become disinclined to make the

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.180
effort at all.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.720
The Pokémon Go Plus wristband was supposed
to change that.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.220
However...

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.880
Plus Disappointment

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.860
When Pokémon Go Plus finally came out, it
just didn't work as players hoped.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:58.500
When the Plus lights up, players can tap the
center button to either catch a Pokémon or

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.330
snag goods from a Pokéstop.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:05.610
Unfortunately, you don't really have a choice
about which of these two things you can do.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:09.930
If there's a wild Pokémon nearby, the Plus
has to catch it before you can access any

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:10.930
Pokéstops.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:16.720
Additionally, the Plus will only use regular
Pokéballs and not your fancy high-yield balls,

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.010
and players report that the catch rate is
far lower than what you'd get if you just

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.490
used the app, leaving many players coming
up empty-handed.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:28.750
You also can't use any items that would make
Pokémon capture easier.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:33.420
And that's not all: you'll still need to be
running the battery-draining app on your phone;

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:38.050
the battery of the Plus is not rechargeable;
and the accessory will constantly disconnect

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.210
from your phone.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.650
You also can't catch types that you haven't
already caught.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.950
You may as well just use your phone.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.310
Or just, like, give up completely.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.240
No Legendary Events

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:55.340
Anyone still playing is holding out for another
undelivered promise from the game's commercials:

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.889
legendary events.

00:06:56.889 --> 00:07:02.090
Even though players still can't directly battle
one another, or even trade Pokémon like civilized

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:08.230
human beings, everyone still wants to be able
to capture a Mewtwo or a Zapdos.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:13.120
Niantic decided to run a Halloween-themed
event to tide people over, and even managed

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.520
to draw back in players who had put the game
aside.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.430
But they've made no statements about releasing
any Legendary Pokémon into the wild.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:25.930
Until then, it's just a handful of people
catching the same things over and over, until

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.400
everyone finally moves on to the next game.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.100
Or just back to trading Pokemon cards.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:36.170
"Are you ready to be the ultimate Pokemon
master trainer?"

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.170
"Yes, I think I am."

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:39.620
Thanks for watching!

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:44.120
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:47.699
And leave us a comment to let us know if you're
still playing Pokemon Go...

